# Subagent Model Selection

Model slugs in the table are agent-adjustable per the rule-governance rule (availability
and pricing shift); the cross-family audit rule below is PROTECTED.

## TEMPORARY OVERRIDE — credit burn (active; user-approved 2026-09-09)

**Until the user lifts this override, cloud swarm agents use these slugs (confirmed
available on Cursor cloud 2026-09-09):**

| Allowed slug | Who |
| --- | --- |
| `cursor-grok-4.5-high` | All swarm main agents (A1–A6) and all their subagents. **Fallback if that slug is missing:** `cursor-grok-4.6-high-fast` |
| `claude-fable-5-thinking-high` | **A1 (tracker) only**, as the swarm's **director**. **Fallback if that slug is missing:** `claude-fable-5-1-thinking-high`. Solely responsible for new overarching tasks, direction, and kill/escalate decisions (see the swarm rule, "Leadership split"). Not for implementation, not for workers |

A1 **must try the primary director slug, then the fallback, in the same turn**. A missing
slug is not a block: keep merging, applying `REQUEST_TRACKER`, and abandoning families
whose kill criteria already fired. Only *new* overarching tasks wait on a successful
director spawn.

Do **not** select Composer, GPT, Gemini, or any other slug while this override is active
— even if the historical defaults table below names them. If a Task tool call requires a
`model` argument, pass `cursor-grok-4.5-high` (or the Grok fallback). Workers never spawn
Claude.

Cross-family audits under this override: suspended by user decision (same-family Grok
reviews are acceptable for the burn); A1's Fable analysis subagent is the only
cross-family check. Dual independent reviewers are paused until the override is lifted.

## Historical defaults (superseded while override is active)

Default choices balancing cost and capability. Start at the cheapest tier that fits the
task; escalate only when a cheaper attempt fails or the task is acceptance-path critical
(spec math, evidence gate, scale/cluster selection). **Ignored while the override above
is active** — remap every row to `cursor-grok-4.5-high` (fallback `cursor-grok-4.6-high-fast`) per that
section.

| Task                                                                                                 | Model                                            | Why                                                                   |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Codebase exploration, file finding, "where is X"                                                     | `composer-2.5-fast`                              | Cheap, fast; exploration is retrieval, not reasoning                  |
| Long-document skims (SI, transcripts), summarization                                                 | `gemini-3.6-flash`                               | Cheap with long context                                               |
| General implementation (well-specified module, tests exist)                                          | `cursor-grok-4.6-high` or `gpt-5.6-terra-medium` | Solid mid-cost coders; enough for spec-following work                 |
| Parallel best-of-N implementation variants                                                           | `gpt-5.6-sol-medium` / `gpt-5.6-terra-medium`    | Cheap enough to run several attempts                                  |
| Hard features, tricky refactors, math-heavy spec work (DM gate, dual flow, Q-score, tau\* selection) | `claude-fable-5.1-thinking-high`                 | Frontier reasoning; acceptance-path code must be right the first time |
| Auditing, red-teaming, reviewing math or proofs                                                      | `gpt-5.6-terra-medium` or `cursor-grok-4.6-high` | **Cross-family rule below**                                           |
| Whole-corpus consistency passes (paper vs SI vs code vs OPEN_ISSUES)                                 | `gemini-3.1-pro`                                 | Long-context deep analysis across many large files                    |

## Cross-family audit rule

Never audit an artifact with the same model family that produced it. The parent agent is
Claude, so reviews of Claude-written code/math go to GPT (`gpt-5.6-terra-medium`) or Grok
(`cursor-grok-4.6-high`); if a GPT subagent wrote it, review with Claude or Gemini.
For high-stakes derivations (SI lemmas, evidence-gate math), use two independent
reviewers from different families and reconcile disagreements yourself.
**(While the temporary override is active, cross-family audits are suspended except A1's Fable analysis subagent — see above.)**

## Delegation patterns for this repo

- Broad context gathering: launch `explore` subagents (specify quick/medium/very thorough);
  parallelize independent areas (e.g. one on `code/proteus/src`, one on the SI).
- Narrow questions (one file, one symbol): do it directly; don't spawn a subagent.
- Implementation subagents must be told: the owning SI section, the relevant rules
  (repo-workflow, proteus-implementation, testing-integrity), the `@awaiting` tests they
  should flip, and the exact pytest subset to run. Subagents don't see this conversation.
- Test-weakening is never an acceptable subagent outcome (testing-integrity rule); state
  that explicitly in red-team and implementation prompts.
